From rct at thompsonclan.org  Thu Mar 13 20:57:14 2014
From: rct at thompsonclan.org (Ryan C. Thompson)
Date: Thu, 13 Mar 2014 12:57:14 -0700
Subject: [BioC] LIMMA/VOOM: Using and Interpretating of natural splines
 coefficients for time series
In-Reply-To: <20140313174025.BDE7914785C@mamba.fhcrc.org>
References: <20140313174025.BDE7914785C@mamba.fhcrc.org>
Message-ID: <53220D9A.3030602@thompsonclan.org>

I'm not very familiar with using natural splines for differential 
expression, but I think you need to be a little more precise about what 
you want. What do you mean by "genes DE at the 48h time point"? DE 
relative to what? If you mean genes DE betweeen 0h and 48h, then maybe 
you want to select whatever linear combination of basis splines yields 
a minimum at 0h and a maximum at 48h and test that. This is just a 
guess, though.

As an alternative strategy, you could linearly interpolate the 48h 
mouse timepoint as the weighted mean of the neighboring time points, 
i.e. "3/4 * MouseTime42 + 1/4 * MouseTime50", since 48 is 3/4 of the 
way from 42 to 50. So then the interaction contrast would be something 
like:

"((3/4 * MouseTime42 + 1/4 * MouseTime50) - MouseTime0) - (HumanTime48 
- HumanTime0)"

By the way, this all assumes that you have a count matrix that includes 
only orthologous gene pairs between mouse and human.

